---
/**
 * THE LAYER SPINE — the site's one signature element (docs/MOTION_SYSTEM.md §2).
 *
 * A single hairline runs down the left margin for the page's full height, and
 * its geometry changes as it descends through the six layers:
 *
 *     models        ·  ·  ·      discrete nodes
 *     computation   ~~~~~~~      probability / phase
 *     digital logic ⌐_⌐_⌐_       clock edges
 *     hardware      ├──┬──┤      routed traces
 *     signals       ∿∿∿∿∿∿∿      continuous wave
 *
 * It is the site's thesis rendered as one line, and it costs almost nothing:
 * inline SVG plus CSS. No canvas, no library, no JavaScript at all — the only
 * thing that moves is a scroll-linked progress indicator, and that is a CSS
 * scroll-driven animation rather than a scroll event handler.
 *
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 * WHAT PASS 2 CHANGED, AND WHY
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *
 * ── 1. The notation now annotates something ────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The previous spine was ONE SVG of `SEG * 5` units stretched over the whole
 * of `<main>`, so each layer occupied a fifth of the DOCUMENT HEIGHT. Since
 * the pinned Technical Worlds traverse alone is ~40% of the document, the
 * "computation" band sat beside Selected Projects and "signals" beside the
 * footer. The glyphs were correct and pointed at nothing — measured as 0px of
 * change over 1.2s, because a reader scrolling a whole section never saw the
 * geometry turn over.
 *
 * Each band is now bound to the SECTION it annotates. Which mapping is honest
 * took some deciding, because the five geometries are the five ENGINEERING
 * LAYERS (`DOMAINS` order) and the five sections are a different list
 * (`SECTION_IDS`: hero, layers, worlds, projects, contact). Pairing them by
 * index would put "signals" beside Contact — the same category error as
 * before, merely re-based.
 *
 * So the mapping is by what each section actually does:
 *
 *   #hero      an implied line. The thesis is STATED here, not traced; the
 *              dotted connector says the descent has not started.
 *   #layers    the full six-geometry sequence, stretched over the ONE section
 *              that names the five layers, in `DOMAINS` order. This is where
 *              the notation earns its place: the glyph beside "digital logic"
 *              is now clock edges, and it is clock edges because that row is
 *              on screen — not because the reader happens to be 40% down the
 *              document.
 *   #worlds    occluded. See the note below; this is a decision, not an
 *              oversight.
 *   #projects  a plain rule. Projects are the evidence, not a layer. Ticks
 *              here would annotate nothing, which is the defect being fixed.
 *   #contact   a plain rule, terminated. The line ends rather than stopping.
 *
 * Registration inside `#layers` is to the SECTION, not to individual rows: the
 * section's own heading and narrative occupy its first stretch, so the five
 * fifths run a little ahead of the five rows. Fixing that would mean putting a
 * band inside each row, which is a change to `LayersSequence.astro`. What was
 * measured as broken — a geometry sitting beside an unrelated section — is
 * fixed; sub-section registration is a further refinement, and it is named
 * here rather than quietly implied.
 *
 * ── Why the Technical Worlds band has no visible spine ─────────────────────
 *
 * Measured, then decided. That band is the one place the ground inverts, and
 * it is full-bleed: the `<section>` paints `--color-ink` from x=0 to the
 * viewport edge, its content measure is `--container-wide` (1408px), and the
 * traverse slides panels with `overflow: visible`, so text reaches x=48 at
 * 1440px and x=32 at 1180px. The spine sits at x=104–144 at 1440px. Rendering
 * it above the ink puts a hairline straight through the panel headings and
 * body copy — screenshotted, and it is as bad as it sounds. Moving it to the
 * page edge would make the site's signature element teleport sideways once per
 * scroll, and no width below ~1500px leaves room for it there anyway.
 *
 * So the line goes behind the ink and comes out below it, which is also what
 * the concept says: MOTION_SYSTEM §3 calls that band "looking into the
 * instrument", and a notebook margin does not exist inside an instrument. The
 * band already reports position with its own progress ticks and per-world
 * accent, so nothing is lost that the reader needed. The band element is still
 * rendered, carrying the plain rule, so the line is continuous the moment that
 * section ever gains a gutter.
 *
 * ── 2. How the binding is done: CSS grid, still zero JavaScript ────────────
 *
 * `<main>` becomes a single-column grid, each section is assigned an explicit
 * row, and each spine band is assigned the SAME row. A grid row is exactly as
 * tall as the section in it, so a band cannot drift out of alignment no matter
 * how the content grows — no measurement, no `ResizeObserver`, no CSS
 * variables written from script.
 *
 * `.spine` itself is `display: contents`, so it contributes no box and its
 * children become grid items of `<main>` directly.
 *
 * Two details make the selectors look stranger than they are:
 *
 *   · `:has(> #worlds)` — ScrollTrigger pins `<section id="worlds">` by
 *     wrapping it in a `.pin-spacer` div at runtime, so after the traverse is
 *     built that section is no longer a child of `<main>`. Matching the
 *     wrapper as well as the section keeps the row assignment correct in both
 *     states. `nth-of-type` would have silently shifted every row after it.
 *   · `main:has(> [data-layer-spine])` — the grid applies only to a page that
 *     actually renders a spine (the homepage), and the specificity of the
 *     `:has()` clears `BaseLayout`'s own `main` rule regardless of the order
 *     the two stylesheets end up in.
 *
 * ── 3. Amplitude and value ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * It was 24px wide and drawn entirely at `--color-rule`, which measured 99% of
 * its pixels at L >= 207 — a 1px non-scaling stroke on a stretched path lands
 * on half-pixels and antialiases away to almost nothing. It is now 40px wide
 * at 1320px and up, and the geometry is drawn at `--color-slate` (5.38:1 on
 * paper) with a 1.5px stroke. `--color-rule` (3.07:1) is kept for the
 * connector and the boundaries, which are the subordinate marks.
 *
 * ── 4. Why the progress marker was not moving ──────────────────────────────
 *
 * `animation: spine-progress linear both` sets `animation-duration` to `0s`
 * through the shorthand, and a scroll-driven animation with a zero duration
 * finishes at progress 0 and never advances. Measured: 0px of travel between
 * scroll top and scroll bottom. The longhands below set
 * `animation-duration: auto`, which is what binds an animation to the whole
 * length of its timeline.
 *
 * ── 5. Why it is still hidden below 1180px ─────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Re-examined rather than inherited. Below that width there is no left margin
 * to put it in: at 1180px the content measure already begins at x=14 and its
 * text at x=46, and the spine occupies 12–38. Forcing it onto a phone would
 * mean drawing it over the text it is meant to annotate — and the mobile
 * composition already carries the per-world coloured left rule that does this
 * job at that size (REDESIGN_DECISIONS, "confirmed good — do not regress").
 * What DID change is that the width is now responsive: 26px in the cramped
 * 1180–1320 range, 40px above it. Amplitude is expressed on the x-axis, so a
 * narrower box compresses the geometry instead of clipping it.
 *
 * ── Two implementation decisions carried over ──────────────────────────────
 *
 * **Why every shape oscillates horizontally.** Each band's SVG stretches
 * vertically to whatever height its section turns out to be
 * (`preserveAspectRatio="none"`), so anything whose *proportions* matter would
 * distort. Expressing amplitude on the x-axis makes the geometry
 * stretch-invariant: vertical stretch only lengthens a period, it never
 * deforms a shape. `vector-effect` keeps the stroke and the dash pattern
 * hairline at any scale, for the same reason.
 *
 * The path data is generated at build time in this frontmatter. It ships as
 * static SVG — nothing below runs in a browser.
 */

import { SECTION_IDS } from '@/config/navigation';

/** Spine centre and layer height, in viewBox units. */
const CX = 20;
const W = 40;
const SEG = 200;
const SEQUENCE_HEIGHT = SEG * 6;

const n = (value: number): string => Number(value.toFixed(2)).toString();

/** A sine that oscillates left-to-right as it descends. */
function sine(y0: number, amp: number, period: number, step = 4): string {
  const points: string[] = [];
  for (let y = y0; y <= y0 + SEG; y += step) {
    const x = CX + amp * Math.sin(((y - y0) / period) * Math.PI * 2);
    points.push(`${n(x)} ${n(y)}`);
  }
  return `M ${points.join(' L ')}`;
}

/** A clock: vertical runs at two rails, joined by instantaneous edges. */
function clock(y0: number, amp: number, period: number): string {
  const half = period / 2;
  const d: string[] = [`M ${CX} ${n(y0)}`];
  let y = y0;
  let x = CX + amp;
  d.push(`L ${n(x)} ${n(y)}`);

  while (y < y0 + SEG) {
    const next = Math.min(y + half, y0 + SEG);
    d.push(`L ${n(x)} ${n(next)}`);
    y = next;
    if (y >= y0 + SEG) break;
    x = x === CX + amp ? CX - amp : CX + amp;
    d.push(`L ${n(x)} ${n(y)}`);
  }

  d.push(`L ${CX} ${n(y0 + SEG)}`);
  return d.join(' ');
}

/** Short horizontal ticks — nodes on the models layer, vias on the routing layer. */
function ticks(points: readonly (readonly [number, number])[], half: number): string {
  return points.map(([x, y]) => `M ${n(x - half)} ${n(y)} L ${n(x + half)} ${n(y)}`).join(' ');
}

/* --- 00 · interface — a completion grid ------------------------------------
   Three marks per row, six rows: the same object the Technical Worlds stage
   for this domain draws, reduced to what survives in a 40-unit channel. It has
   to read as a grid and not as the models layer below it, which is why these
   are short marks in three columns where `nodes` is one long mark on the
   centreline. -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const GRID_XS = [CX - 9, CX, CX + 9] as const;
const GRID_YS = [30, 60, 90, 120, 150, 180] as const;
const completionGrid = ticks(
  GRID_YS.flatMap((y) => GRID_XS.map((x) => [x, y] as const)),
  3.5,
);

/* --- 01 · models — discrete nodes on a dotted connector -------------------- */
const NODE_YS = [226, 258, 290, 322, 354, 386] as const;
const nodes = ticks(
  NODE_YS.map((y) => [CX, y] as const),
  10,
);

/* --- 02 · computation — interference, four full cycles --------------------- */
const computation = sine(SEG * 2, 11, 50);

/* --- 03 · digital logic — clock edges -------------------------------------- */
const logic = clock(SEG * 3, 9, 50);

/* --- 04 · hardware — routed traces. Vertices are vertical or exactly 45°. -- */
const ROUTE = [
  [20, 800],
  [20, 812],
  [31, 823],
  [31, 846],
  [10, 867],
  [10, 884],
  [27, 901],
  [27, 920],
  [15, 932],
  [15, 958],
  [25, 968],
  [25, 983],
  [20, 988],
  [20, 1000],
] as const satisfies readonly (readonly [number, number])[];

const hardware = `M ${ROUTE.map(([x, y]) => `${n(x)} ${n(y)}`).join(' L ')}`;
const vias = ticks(
  [
    [31, 834],
    [10, 875],
    [27, 910],
    [15, 945],
  ] as const,
  5,
);

/* --- 05 · signals — a denser continuous wave, eight full cycles ------------ */
const signals = sine(SEG * 5, 8, 25, 3);

/* --- the spine's own structure -------------------------------------------- */

/** The connector every band shares: one vertical run down the centre. */
const connector = (height: number): string => `M ${CX} 0 L ${CX} ${n(height)}`;

/** Boundaries between the six layers of a sequence band. */
const boundaries = ticks(
  [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map((i) => [CX, SEG * i] as const),
  15,
);

/** Origin mark at the head of the spine, and its terminator at the foot. */
const ORIGIN_HEIGHT = 120;
const originTick = ticks([[CX, 6]] as const, 15);
const terminatorTick = ticks([[CX, ORIGIN_HEIGHT - 6]] as const, 15);

/**
 * The section each band is bound to, in `SECTION_IDS` order. Destructured from
 * the config rather than written out: `SECTION_IDS` is a fixed-length readonly
 * tuple, so dropping a section makes this line a compile error instead of
 * leaving a band bound to nothing. Renaming one is not caught here — the row
 * selectors in the stylesheet below carry the same five ids, and CSS cannot
 * read a TypeScript constant. `tests/anchors.test.ts` already fails if a
 * section id loses its anchor, which is the same rename.
 */
const [HERO, LAYERS, WORLDS, PROJECTS, CONTACT] = SECTION_IDS;
---

<div class="spine" data-layer-spine aria-hidden="true">
  {/* 00 — the thesis is stated, not yet traced. */}
  <div class="spine__band" data-band={HERO}>
    <svg class="spine__svg" viewBox={`0 0 ${W} ${ORIGIN_HEIGHT}`} preserveAspectRatio="none" focusable="false">
      <path class="spine__connector" d={connector(ORIGIN_HEIGHT)}></path>
      <path class="spine__boundary" d={originTick}></path>
    </svg>
  </div>

  {/* 01 — the six layers, named. One geometry per sixth, in DOMAINS order. */}
  <div class="spine__band" data-band={LAYERS}>
    <svg class="spine__svg" viewBox={`0 0 ${W} ${SEQUENCE_HEIGHT}`} preserveAspectRatio="none" focusable="false">
      <path class="spine__connector" d={connector(SEG)}></path>
      <path class="spine__stroke" d={completionGrid}></path>
      <path class="spine__stroke" d={nodes}></path>
      <path class="spine__stroke" d={computation}></path>
      <path class="spine__stroke" d={logic}></path>
      <path class="spine__stroke" d={hardware}></path>
      <path class="spine__stroke" d={vias}></path>
      <path class="spine__stroke" d={signals}></path>
      <path class="spine__boundary" d={boundaries}></path>
    </svg>
  </div>

  {
    /* 02 — the run continues behind the ink ground. See the note in the header:
      this band is deliberately occluded, and is kept so that the line is
      continuous the moment that band ever stops being full-bleed. */
  }
  <div class="spine__band" data-band={WORLDS}>
    <svg class="spine__svg" viewBox={`0 0 ${W} ${ORIGIN_HEIGHT}`} preserveAspectRatio="none" focusable="false">
      <path class="spine__rule" d={connector(ORIGIN_HEIGHT)}></path>
    </svg>
  </div>

  {/* 03 / 04 — evidence and close. Not layers, so no notation is claimed. */}
  <div class="spine__band" data-band={PROJECTS}>
    <svg class="spine__svg" viewBox={`0 0 ${W} ${ORIGIN_HEIGHT}`} preserveAspectRatio="none" focusable="false">
      <path class="spine__rule" d={connector(ORIGIN_HEIGHT)}></path>
    </svg>
  </div>

  <div class="spine__band" data-band={CONTACT}>
    <svg class="spine__svg" viewBox={`0 0 ${W} ${ORIGIN_HEIGHT}`} preserveAspectRatio="none" focusable="false">
      <path class="spine__rule" d={connector(ORIGIN_HEIGHT)}></path>
      <path class="spine__boundary" d={terminatorTick}></path>
    </svg>
  </div>

  <div class="spine__track"><span class="spine__marker"></span></div>
</div>

<style>
  /* --- placement -----------------------------------------------------------
     Hidden by default. Everything below, including the grid imposed on
     `<main>`, is switched on together at the one breakpoint — so a viewport
     that never shows the spine never has its layout touched either.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .spine,
  .spine__track {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (min-width: 1180px) {
    /* `<main>` becomes the single-column grid the bands share with the
       sections. `:has(> [data-layer-spine])` scopes it to pages that render a
       spine and outranks BaseLayout's own bare `main` rule. */
    :global(main:has(> [data-layer-spine])) {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      /* Five EXPLICIT rows, one per `SECTION_IDS` entry. Declaring them rather
         than letting them be implicit is what makes `grid-row: 1 / -1` on the
         progress track mean "the whole run": `-1` resolves against the explicit
         grid only, so with implicit rows the track collapsed to the height of
         row 1 and the marker travelled 736px of 8676. Measured. */
      grid-template-rows: repeat(5, auto);
    }

    /* Every laid-out child shares the one column; the sections and the bands
       are then paired by explicit row below. `.pin-spacer` is matched through
       `:has(> #id)` because ScrollTrigger wraps the pinned section at runtime. */
    :global(main:has(> [data-layer-spine]) > *) {
      grid-column: 1;
      min-width: 0;
    }

    .spine {
      /* No box of its own: the bands are grid items of `<main>` directly. */
      display: contents;
      color: var(--color-rule);
    }

    /* `grid-column` has to be declared here rather than picked up from the
       `main > *` rule above: `display: contents` promotes these to grid ITEMS
       of `<main>`, but they remain DOM grandchildren, so no `main > *`
       selector reaches them. Without it they auto-place into implicit columns
       and squeeze the sections — measured, not theorised. */
    .spine__band,
    .spine__track {
      position: relative;
      z-index: 0;
      grid-column: 1;
      justify-self: start;
      width: 26px;
      margin-inline-start: clamp(0.75rem, calc((100vw - var(--container-content)) / 2 - 4.5rem), 7rem);
      pointer-events: none;
      color: var(--color-rule);
    }

    .spine__track {
      display: block;
    }

    @media (min-width: 1320px) {
      .spine__band,
      .spine__track {
        width: 40px;
      }
    }

    /* Absolute, so a band contributes nothing to the height of the row it
       shares with its section — the section alone sizes the row. */
    .spine__svg {
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      width: 100%;
      height: 100%;
    }
  }

  /* --- band <-> section row pairing ---------------------------------------
     The whole point of the rewrite. Row N holds section N and the band bound
     to it, so the band is exactly as tall as what it annotates.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  @media (min-width: 1180px) {
    :global(main:has(> [data-layer-spine]) > :is(#hero, :has(> #hero))),
    .spine__band[data-band='hero'] {
      grid-row: 1;
    }

    :global(main:has(> [data-layer-spine]) > :is(#layers, :has(> #layers))),
    .spine__band[data-band='layers'] {
      grid-row: 2;
    }

    :global(main:has(> [data-layer-spine]) > :is(#worlds, :has(> #worlds))),
    .spine__band[data-band='worlds'] {
      grid-row: 3;
    }

    :global(main:has(> [data-layer-spine]) > :is(#projects, :has(> #projects))),
    .spine__band[data-band='projects'] {
      grid-row: 4;
    }

    :global(main:has(> [data-layer-spine]) > :is(#contact, :has(> #contact))),
    .spine__band[data-band='contact'] {
      grid-row: 5;
    }

    /* The progress track spans the whole run, so the marker is continuous
       across the five bands — except where the ink band paints over it, which
       is the one deliberate interruption (see the header note). */
    .spine__track {
      grid-row: 1 / -1;
    }
  }

  /* --- marks ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .spine__stroke,
  .spine__connector,
  .spine__boundary,
  .spine__rule {
    fill: none;
    stroke: currentColor;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    /* Keeps hairlines hairline, and dash patterns uniform, at any stretch. */
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  /* The geometry itself, at the value the review asked for: 5.38:1 on paper
     rather than the ~1.5:1 a 1px --color-rule stroke was antialiasing down to. */
  .spine__stroke {
    stroke: var(--color-slate);
    stroke-width: 1.5;
  }

  /* The models layer is discrete: the connector between nodes is implied. */
  .spine__connector {
    stroke-width: 1;
    stroke-dasharray: 1 9;
  }

  .spine__rule,
  .spine__boundary {
    stroke-width: 1;
  }

  /* --- the one moving part -------------------------------------------------
     A scroll-driven CSS animation. No scroll listener exists anywhere in this
     component; where `animation-timeline` is unsupported the marker simply
     never renders and the spine is static, which is a complete state rather
     than a broken one.

     Longhands, not the `animation` shorthand: the shorthand resets
     `animation-duration` to `0s`, and a scroll-driven animation of zero
     duration sits at progress 0 forever. `auto` is what binds it to the whole
     length of the timeline. Measured before and after — 0px of travel became
     the full run of `<main>`.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .spine__marker {
    display: none;
  }

  @supports (animation-timeline: scroll()) {
    .spine__marker {
      display: block;
      position: absolute;
      left: 0;
      right: 0;
      top: 0;
      height: var(--border-rule);
      background-color: var(--color-accent);
      animation-name: spine-progress;
      animation-duration: auto;
      animation-timing-function: linear;
      animation-fill-mode: both;
      animation-timeline: scroll(root block);
    }
  }

  @keyframes spine-progress {
    from {
      top: 0;
    }
    to {
      top: calc(100% - var(--border-rule));
    }
  }

  /* Reduced motion: the spine stays exactly as it is, minus the indicator.
     Declared last so it wins over the @supports block above. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .spine__marker {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
